1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,880
Welcome to episode four.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:05,081
So far we've treated fuels as equations.

3
00:00:05,121 --> 00:00:11,722
Today we look at the fuels themselves:
where they come from, how biofuels are made,

4
00:00:11,762 --> 00:00:15,203
and what the carbon in
them does over time.

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,721
First, two quick checks from last episode.

6
00:00:18,761 --> 00:00:22,408
Does a catalyst change
the delta H of a reaction?

7
00:00:22,448 --> 00:00:28,654
And if two moles of hydrogen burning has
delta H equal to negative five hundred and

8
00:00:28,694 --> 00:00:34,449
seventy-two kilojoules, what is the
molar enthalpy of combustion of hydrogen?

9
00:00:34,489 --> 00:00:35,768
Pause and answer.

10
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:49,079
A catalyst lowers the activation
energy but does not change delta H.

11
00:00:49,119 --> 00:00:54,576
And the molar enthalpy of combustion of
hydrogen is negative two hundred and

12
00:00:54,616 --> 00:00:58,844
eighty-six kilojoules per mole
of hydrogen: half the value,

13
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:01,706
because the equation was for two moles.

14
00:01:02,666 --> 00:01:06,771
A fuel is a substance that
undergoes a chemical reaction,

15
00:01:06,811 --> 00:01:12,369
usually combustion with oxygen, to
release useful energy, most often as heat.

16
00:01:12,409 --> 00:01:18,113
The useful part matters: we choose fuels
because the energy can be captured and

17
00:01:18,153 --> 00:01:18,826
used.

18
00:01:18,866 --> 00:01:22,620
The fossil fuels are coal,
natural gas and petrol.

19
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:24,289
Coal is mostly carbon.

20
00:01:24,329 --> 00:01:26,566
Natural gas is mostly methane.

21
00:01:26,606 --> 00:01:32,828
Petrol is a mixture of liquid hydrocarbons
refined from crude oil, such as octane.

22
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:38,917
Fossil fuels formed from the remains of
organisms buried and transformed by heat

23
00:01:38,957 --> 00:01:41,508
and pressure over millions of years.

24
00:01:41,548 --> 00:01:44,890
We're using them far
faster than they can form,

25
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:48,456
so on any human timescale
they are non-renewable.

26
00:01:49,399 --> 00:01:51,275
Compare two timescales.

27
00:01:51,315 --> 00:01:55,023
A fossil fuel took
millions of years to form.

28
00:01:55,063 --> 00:02:01,103
A biofuel is made from biomass, such
as crops, plant waste or animal fat,

29
00:02:01,143 --> 00:02:05,683
that can be regrown or replaced
within months or years.

30
00:02:05,723 --> 00:02:11,637
A fuel is renewable if it is replenished
at least as fast as we use it.

31
00:02:11,999 --> 00:02:14,260
There are three biofuels to know.

32
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:18,234
Biogas is a mixture of mainly
methane and carbon dioxide,

33
00:02:18,274 --> 00:02:22,348
made when microbes break down
organic waste without oxygen.

34
00:02:22,388 --> 00:02:25,695
Bioethanol is ethanol made
by fermenting sugars.

35
00:02:25,735 --> 00:02:30,994
And biodiesel is made by reacting plant
oils or animal fats with an alcohol,

36
00:02:31,034 --> 00:02:33,823
in a process called transesterification.

37
00:02:34,199 --> 00:02:36,152
Notice something important.

38
00:02:36,192 --> 00:02:41,394
Methane from biogas and methane from
natural gas are the same molecule.

39
00:02:41,434 --> 00:02:47,078
Burning one mole of either releases the
same energy and forms the same carbon

40
00:02:47,118 --> 00:02:47,668
dioxide.

41
00:02:47,708 --> 00:02:53,540
What differs is where the carbon came
from, and how quickly it can be replaced.

42
00:02:54,532 --> 00:02:56,640
Let's follow some carbon atoms.

43
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:59,897
They start in carbon
dioxide in the atmosphere.

44
00:02:59,937 --> 00:03:05,302
During photosynthesis, a plant uses energy
from sunlight to build those carbon

45
00:03:05,342 --> 00:03:06,658
atoms into glucose.

46
00:03:06,999 --> 00:03:10,046
The glucose can be fermented into ethanol.

47
00:03:10,086 --> 00:03:13,133
The same carbon atoms are now in the fuel,

48
00:03:13,173 --> 00:03:17,290
with some leaving as carbon
dioxide during fermentation.

49
00:03:17,665 --> 00:03:23,597
When the ethanol burns, its carbon
atoms return to the atmosphere as carbon

50
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:27,578
dioxide, and the next crop
can take them up again.

51
00:03:27,618 --> 00:03:33,749
Over a full cycle, this carbon is recycled
on a timescale of months or years.

52
00:03:34,132 --> 00:03:36,541
Burning a fossil fuel is different.

53
00:03:36,581 --> 00:03:40,808
Its carbon was locked away
underground for millions of years.

54
00:03:40,848 --> 00:03:44,795
Releasing it adds extra carbon
dioxide to the atmosphere,

55
00:03:44,835 --> 00:03:47,913
faster than natural
processes can remove it.

56
00:03:48,265 --> 00:03:52,454
But careful: the biofuel cycle
isn't automatically carbon neutral.

57
00:03:52,494 --> 00:03:57,835
Growing, harvesting, processing and transporting
the fuel can use fossil energy too.

58
00:03:57,875 --> 00:04:01,399
We'll look at those wider
boundaries in episode twelve.

59
00:04:02,399 --> 00:04:08,167
Photosynthesis: six carbon dioxide plus six
water forms one glucose plus six oxygen.

60
00:04:08,207 --> 00:04:13,877
Check the atoms: six carbons, twelve
hydrogens, and eighteen oxygens on each side.

61
00:04:14,265 --> 00:04:16,206
Photosynthesis is endothermic.

62
00:04:16,246 --> 00:04:18,385
The products, glucose and oxygen,

63
00:04:18,425 --> 00:04:21,884
sit higher in enthalpy than
carbon dioxide and water.

64
00:04:21,924 --> 00:04:26,505
That energy doesn't appear from
nowhere: it is absorbed from sunlight.

65
00:04:26,545 --> 00:04:29,648
Light energy is converted
into chemical energy.

66
00:04:29,999 --> 00:04:33,443
Cellular respiration is
the reverse overall change.

67
00:04:33,483 --> 00:04:37,541
Glucose reacts with oxygen to
form carbon dioxide and water,

68
00:04:37,581 --> 00:04:42,597
releasing roughly two point eight
thousand kilojoules per mole of glucose.

69
00:04:42,637 --> 00:04:47,857
As we saw in episode two, that energy
is released because the bonds formed in

70
00:04:47,897 --> 00:04:52,883
carbon dioxide and water are stronger,
not because glucose's bonds break.

71
00:04:53,865 --> 00:04:59,167
Fermentation: glucose in solution forms
two ethanol and two carbon dioxide.

72
00:04:59,207 --> 00:05:00,307
Watch the atoms.

73
00:05:00,347 --> 00:05:04,011
Six carbons, twelve hydrogens
and six oxygens go in,

74
00:05:04,051 --> 00:05:07,826
and exactly the same atoms
come out, just rearranged.

75
00:05:08,198 --> 00:05:13,455
This reaction is catalysed by enzymes
from yeast, in the absence of oxygen,

76
00:05:13,495 --> 00:05:17,693
at a warm temperature, around
thirty to thirty-five degrees.

77
00:05:17,733 --> 00:05:20,730
Enzymes are proteins
with a specific shape.

78
00:05:20,770 --> 00:05:25,179
At a very high temperature, their
shape is permanently damaged.

79
00:05:25,219 --> 00:05:30,022
We say the enzyme is denatured, and
the fermentation slows or stops.

80
00:05:30,398 --> 00:05:33,570
Fermentation only produces
a dilute ethanol solution,

81
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:35,872
around ten to fifteen percent ethanol,

82
00:05:35,912 --> 00:05:39,144
because the yeast can't
survive higher concentrations.

83
00:05:39,184 --> 00:05:42,758
So we separate and concentrate
the ethanol by distillation.

84
00:05:43,132 --> 00:05:46,507
Distillation uses the
difference in boiling points.

85
00:05:46,547 --> 00:05:50,994
Ethanol boils at about seventy-eight
degrees, water at one hundred.

86
00:05:51,034 --> 00:05:56,083
When the mixture is heated, the vapour
is richer in ethanol than the liquid,

87
00:05:56,123 --> 00:05:59,097
and it's cooled and
collected in a condenser.

88
00:05:59,137 --> 00:06:01,843
The distillate is much more concentrated,

89
00:06:01,883 --> 00:06:05,392
but ordinary distillation
can't produce pure ethanol:

90
00:06:05,432 --> 00:06:08,446
it reaches about
ninety-five percent at most.

91
00:06:09,398 --> 00:06:13,622
Practice question Q zero
seven is worth six marks.

92
00:06:13,662 --> 00:06:15,965
Pause the video and try it.

93
00:06:31,398 --> 00:06:37,459
First, the balanced equation: one glucose
forms two ethanol and two carbon dioxide,

94
00:06:37,499 --> 00:06:42,825
with glucose and ethanol in aqueous
solution and carbon dioxide as a gas.

95
00:06:42,865 --> 00:06:46,834
That ratio, one to two to
two, drives everything else.

96
00:06:47,198 --> 00:06:48,150
Now predict.

97
00:06:48,190 --> 00:06:52,366
Glucose has a molar mass
of one hundred and eighty,

98
00:06:52,406 --> 00:06:58,402
and we have two hundred and seventy
grams, so about one and a half moles.

99
00:06:58,442 --> 00:07:02,122
That could make up to
three moles of ethanol,

100
00:07:02,162 --> 00:07:05,594
about one hundred and forty grams at most.

101
00:07:05,634 --> 00:07:11,918
With a yield below one hundred percent,
expect a bit over one hundred grams.

102
00:07:12,264 --> 00:07:18,511
Amount of glucose: two hundred and seventy
point zero grams divided by one hundred

103
00:07:18,551 --> 00:07:24,108
and eighty point zero grams per mole,
which is one point five zero moles.

104
00:07:24,148 --> 00:07:30,012
The theoretical amount of ethanol is
twice that: three point zero zero moles.

105
00:07:30,052 --> 00:07:32,199
The same for carbon dioxide.

106
00:07:32,598 --> 00:07:37,895
Only seventy-eight point zero percent
of that theoretical conversion happens,

107
00:07:37,935 --> 00:07:43,647
so multiply once: zero point seven eight
zero times three point zero zero gives two

108
00:07:43,687 --> 00:07:49,509
point three four moles of ethanol, and two
point three four moles of carbon dioxide.

109
00:07:49,864 --> 00:07:51,377
Now convert to mass.

110
00:07:51,417 --> 00:07:57,589
Ethanol: two point three four moles times
forty-six point zero grams per mole is

111
00:07:57,629 --> 00:08:03,412
one hundred and seven point six four,
so about one hundred and eight grams.

112
00:08:03,452 --> 00:08:09,857
Carbon dioxide: two point three four times
forty-four point zero is one hundred and

113
00:08:09,897 --> 00:08:14,282
two point nine six, so about
one hundred and three grams.

114
00:08:14,322 --> 00:08:16,186
Both fit the prediction.

115
00:08:16,531 --> 00:08:18,654
For distillation, a full answer is:

116
00:08:18,694 --> 00:08:23,660
distillation separates and concentrates
the ethanol from the aqueous fermentation

117
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:28,891
mixture, using the difference in volatility,
or boiling point, of ethanol and water.

118
00:08:29,264 --> 00:08:33,421
Marks: one for the equation,
one for the two to one ratio,

119
00:08:33,461 --> 00:08:38,703
one for applying the yield, two for
the masses, and one for distillation.

120
00:08:38,743 --> 00:08:42,683
The traps: a one to one ratio,
which halves the answer;

121
00:08:42,723 --> 00:08:45,360
applying seventy-eight percent twice;

122
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:49,484
or describing distillation as
if it produced the ethanol.

123
00:08:49,524 --> 00:08:52,635
Distillation separates;
fermentation makes.

124
00:08:53,596 --> 00:08:56,236
Biogas comes from anaerobic digestion.

125
00:08:56,276 --> 00:08:59,762
Organic waste, like manure,
food scraps or sewage,

126
00:08:59,802 --> 00:09:02,230
is sealed in a tank without oxygen.

127
00:09:02,270 --> 00:09:05,192
Microbes break it down over days to weeks,

128
00:09:05,232 --> 00:09:09,212
producing a gas that's mainly
methane and carbon dioxide.

129
00:09:09,252 --> 00:09:13,765
The leftover solid, called digestate,
can be used as fertiliser.

130
00:09:14,130 --> 00:09:18,303
Biodiesel starts from fats and
oils, which are triglycerides.

131
00:09:18,343 --> 00:09:23,966
A triglyceride is three fatty acid chains
attached to a glycerol backbone by ester

132
00:09:24,006 --> 00:09:24,756
links.

133
00:09:24,796 --> 00:09:30,140
In transesterification, the triglyceride
reacts with three molecules of an alcohol,

134
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:34,161
usually methanol, with a catalyst
such as potassium hydroxide.

135
00:09:34,201 --> 00:09:36,561
Each fatty acid chain swaps partners:

136
00:09:36,601 --> 00:09:39,868
it leaves the glycerol and
attaches to the alcohol.

137
00:09:39,908 --> 00:09:44,927
So one triglyceride plus three alcohol
molecules gives three fatty acid methyl

138
00:09:44,967 --> 00:09:48,275
esters, which are the
biodiesel, plus one glycerol.

139
00:09:48,663 --> 00:09:53,774
For this series, that ratio is the
key: one triglyceride, three alcohol,

140
00:09:53,814 --> 00:09:55,705
three esters, one glycerol.

141
00:09:55,745 --> 00:10:00,713
The detailed organic chemistry belongs
to the organic chemistry topic;

142
00:10:00,753 --> 00:10:06,762
here we just need to recognise the reaction
and why the product is a renewable fuel.

143
00:10:07,728 --> 00:10:11,644
Practice question Q zero
eight is worth four marks.

144
00:10:11,684 --> 00:10:14,709
Pause the video and write your answers.

145
00:10:30,128 --> 00:10:30,628
Part a.

146
00:10:30,668 --> 00:10:36,638
Both fuels are pure methane, so per mole
burned under the same conditions they

147
00:10:36,678 --> 00:10:42,032
release the same energy and produce
the same amount of carbon dioxide:

148
00:10:42,072 --> 00:10:45,653
one mole of carbon dioxide
per mole of methane.

149
00:10:45,693 --> 00:10:49,469
The molecule doesn't
remember where it came from.

150
00:10:49,862 --> 00:10:50,320
Part b.

151
00:10:50,360 --> 00:10:53,310
Methane from natural gas is non-renewable:

152
00:10:53,350 --> 00:10:58,649
it formed over millions of years and
is used far faster than it's replaced.

153
00:10:58,689 --> 00:11:02,635
Biomethane from recently grown
plant waste is renewable,

154
00:11:02,675 --> 00:11:06,306
because its feedstock
regrows on a human timescale.

155
00:11:06,662 --> 00:11:07,085
Part c.

156
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:09,467
Renewable doesn't prove sustainable.

157
00:11:09,507 --> 00:11:12,511
Methane can leak from
digesters and pipelines,

158
00:11:12,551 --> 00:11:17,076
and methane is a much more potent
greenhouse gas than carbon dioxide.

159
00:11:17,116 --> 00:11:21,774
Processing also uses energy, and
growing feedstock uses land and water.

160
00:11:21,814 --> 00:11:24,527
Sustainability needs the whole lifecycle.

161
00:11:24,862 --> 00:11:29,374
One mark for part a, two for
part b, and one for part c.

162
00:11:29,414 --> 00:11:35,878
The trap is saying that biomethane doesn't
produce carbon dioxide when it burns.

163
00:11:35,918 --> 00:11:38,642
It does, exactly as much per mole.

164
00:11:38,682 --> 00:11:44,616
The difference is that its carbon was
recently taken from the atmosphere.

165
00:11:44,995 --> 00:11:47,190
So, the checkpoint for this episode:

166
00:11:47,230 --> 00:11:50,232
renewable means replenished
on a human timescale.

167
00:11:50,272 --> 00:11:55,052
It doesn't mean emission-free, and it
doesn't automatically mean sustainable.

168
00:11:55,995 --> 00:11:56,502
To recap.

169
00:11:56,542 --> 00:11:59,298
Coal, natural gas and
petrol are fossil fuels:

170
00:11:59,338 --> 00:12:02,763
non-renewable because they
formed over millions of years.

171
00:12:02,803 --> 00:12:05,073
Biogas comes from anaerobic digestion,

172
00:12:05,113 --> 00:12:08,355
bioethanol from fermentation
followed by distillation,

173
00:12:08,395 --> 00:12:11,759
and biodiesel from
transesterification of fats and oils.

174
00:12:11,799 --> 00:12:15,284
They're renewable because their
feedstocks regrow quickly,

175
00:12:15,324 --> 00:12:17,999
but renewable isn't the
same as sustainable.

176
00:12:18,395 --> 00:12:19,282
Closing recall.

177
00:12:19,322 --> 00:12:22,741
Write the balanced equation
for fermentation of glucose,

178
00:12:22,781 --> 00:12:25,376
and say what distillation does afterwards.

179
00:12:35,795 --> 00:12:39,099
Glucose forms two ethanol
plus two carbon dioxide.

180
00:12:39,139 --> 00:12:42,911
Distillation then separates
and concentrates the ethanol,

181
00:12:42,951 --> 00:12:44,918
using its lower boiling point.

182
00:12:44,958 --> 00:12:48,101
Next episode: food as a
chemical energy source.
